One-minute penalty for each missed target."]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Course Length: 20,000 m" [:br {}] "Height Differential: 65 m" [:br {}] "Maximum Climb: 17 m" [:br {}] "Shooting 1: Prone at 4.0 km, 50 m range" [:br {}] "Shooting 2: Standing at 8.0 km, 50 m range" [:br {}] "Shooting 3: Prone at 12.0 km, 50 m range" [:br {}] "Shooting 4: Standing at 16.0 km, 50 m range" [:br {}] "Total Climbing: 620 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/84154"} "Ole Einar Bjørndalen"] " was the defending champion, having won all four gold medals at Salt Lake City, and he had also won four golds at the 2005 World Championships, although he had missed out in this event. The heavy favorite, he had been sick with a severe cold in January that cost him several days of training. Bjørndalen started 57th with the lead held by German " [:a {:href "/athletes/101373"} "Michael Greis"] ". Greis had started 38th, and missed only one target to take the lead, posting what would be the fifth-best ski time. Bjørndalen outskied everybody, posting the fastest ski time, but he missed two targets and that was the margin as Greis held on to win the gold medal, Bjørndalen placing second. Greis would later win the mass start race and add a gold medal in the relay to give him three gold medals in Torino. Third place went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/86432"} "Halvard Hanevold"] " who had won the gold medal in the event in 1998. A major surprise was Slovakia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/101208"} "Marek Matiaško"] ", whose best major international finish had been sevent at the 2000 Worlds. He was in medal contention until the final shooting when he missed to dropp down to fifth place."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Adjusted Time"] [:th {} "Misses"] [:th {} "Skiing"] [:th {} "Shooting 1 Misses"] [:th {} "Shooting 1"] [:th {} "Shooting 2 Misses"] [:th {} "Shooting 2"] [